nodecode-web-local is a rewrite of enzo-adami's enzo-adami/hermes-plugin-web-local:

  https://github.com/enzo-adami/hermes-plugin-web-local
  commit cef74b40c3ff379a1d5c63264ef99e8f2c9c5529
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Enzo Adami (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin was one web-extract provider for Hermes. It fetched
each URL from the agent's machine with httpx, followed redirects by hand at most five
hops, re-checked the SSRF rule and the website policy on every hop, capped a body at
10MB and a URL at 60 seconds inside the caller's batch budget, and reduced the page
with trafilatura, answering readable text, a title, a metadata mapping and one
result per URL -- a failed page as an error item rather than an exception. This cell
keeps that shape in Common Lisp: the same per-URL fetch with an honest product user
agent, the same content-type gate (HTML, XML and plain text; anything else names
itself), the same 10MB cap, the same batch deadline that spends what the caller
allowed, the same per-page error line, and the same metadata -- title, site,
byline, publish date -- with the description quoted under the page's own line. The
reduction is cl-ppcre's in place of trafilatura, so headings, list items, links,
table cells and code blocks come out as markdown rather than through a readability
library. Hermes' extract backend registry has no Nodecode counterpart, so the same
reader is exposed as the local_extract tool, the (web-local:extract ...) verb and
the /web-local command.

What was left out and why: trafilatura (a Python library, not vendored here, so the
reduction is coarser on an unusual page); the plugin's `format: "html"` output (a
page here is markdown or plain text); the check on every redirect hop, because the
image's one HTTP exchange follows up to five redirects itself, so the private-network
check runs on the address the caller gave; and the resolver-level part of the SSRF
rule, so a hostname that resolves into a private network is not caught.
